---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const link = 'text-primary underline';
const strong = 'font-medium text-foreground';

/** Each online service the app contacts: what it is for, what it receives, and its own privacy policy. */
const services = [
  {
    name: 'Place search: Photon (komoot) and, as a fallback, Nominatim (OpenStreetMap Foundation)',
    sends:
      'The text you search for (or a calendar event’s place, see below), your approximate position so that nearby results come first, and the coordinates of a point you pick on the map, to find its name.',
    policies: [
      { label: 'komoot', href: 'https://www.komoot.com/privacy' },
      { label: 'OpenStreetMap Foundation', href: 'https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Routing: Valhalla and OSRM on the servers of FOSSGIS e.V.; TomTom only if you enter your own TomTom key',
    sends: 'The coordinates of the trip’s start, stops and destination. TomTom also receives your key and the departure time.',
    policies: [
      { label: 'FOSSGIS e.V.', href: 'https://www.fossgis.de/datenschutzerkl%C3%A4rung/' },
      { label: 'TomTom', href: 'https://www.tomtom.com/privacy/' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Weather and elevation: Open-Meteo',
    sends: 'Points along the route and the travel dates.',
    policies: [{ label: 'Open-Meteo', href: 'https://open-meteo.com/en/terms#privacy' }],
  },
  {
    name: 'Roadworks: the Autobahn API of Die Autobahn GmbH des Bundes',
    sends: 'Only the numbers of the motorways on your route (for example “A5”).',
    policies: [{ label: 'Die Autobahn GmbH', href: 'https://www.autobahn.de/datenschutz' }],
  },
  {
    name: 'Map: OpenFreeMap',
    sends: 'Requests for the map tiles of the area shown on the screen.',
    policies: [{ label: 'OpenFreeMap', href: 'https://openfreemap.org/privacy/' }],
  },
  {
    name: 'LPG prices: mylpg.eu, clever-tanken.de and gas-tankstellen.de',
    sends:
      'mylpg.eu: the app downloads its list of German LPG stations and the pages of stations you link; nothing about your position. clever-tanken.de: the positions of LPG stations near your route or near you, each as a price list within 2 km, and station pages. gas-tankstellen.de: the positions of LPG stations near your route or near you, each as a search within 25 km. Each site can be switched off in the app’s settings.',
    policies: [
      { label: 'mylpg.eu', href: 'https://www.mylpg.eu/legal/' },
      { label: 'clever-tanken.de', href: 'https://www.clever-tanken.de/datenschutz' },
      { label: 'gas-tankstellen.de', href: 'https://www.gas-tankstellen.de/datenschutzerklaerung.php' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Petrol price: Tankerkönig, only when the app has a Tankerkönig key (yours, or one built into the app)',
    sends: 'The position of the trip’s start, to find petrol stations near it, and the key.',
    policies: [{ label: 'Tankerkönig', href: 'https://www.tankerkoenig.de/datenschutz' }],
  },
  {
    name: 'Car parks and the station list: the Overpass API (overpass-api.de of FOSSGIS e.V.; if it does not answer, the mirrors overpass.kumi.systems, overpass.private.coffee and maps.mail.ru, in that order)',
    sends:
      'The destination’s position, to find up to three car parks near it. Updating the station list (in the settings) downloads all LPG stations in Germany and sends no position.',
    policies: [{ label: 'FOSSGIS e.V.', href: 'https://www.fossgis.de/datenschutzerkl%C3%A4rung/' }],
  },
  {
    name: 'Google Maps',
    sends:
      'When you tap Navigate, the app hands the coordinates and name of the stop or destination (for the whole trip: the start, stops and destination) to Google Maps, or to another map app when Google Maps is not installed; with no map app at all, it opens the place on openstreetmap.org in your browser. When you share a place from Google Maps to the app as a short link (maps.app.goo.gl), the app opens that link once, following only its redirects, to read the coordinates.',
    policies: [{ label: 'Google', href: 'https://policies.google.com/privacy' }],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="LPG Trip Planner Privacy Policy · Arash Kadkhodaei"
  description="Privacy policy of the LPG Trip Planner Android app: what stays on your phone, which online services the app contacts and what they receive, and the optional Google Drive backup."
  noindex
>
  <section class="py-24">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-3xl">
      <div class="text-center mb-12 animate-fade-up animate-delay-1">
        <p class="text-sm font-mono text-secondary tracking-widest uppercase mb-2">LPG Trip Planner</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Privacy Policy</h1>
        <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-linear-to-r from-primary to-secondary mx-auto rounded-full mb-6" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Effective date: <time datetime="2026-10-10">10 October 2026</time></p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground animate-fade-up animate-delay-2">

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">1. In short</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <a href="/lpg-trip/" class={link}>LPG Trip Planner</a> keeps your data on your phone. It needs no account and
            has no ads, no analytics and no telemetry. I, the developer, run no server for it and receive nothing from it.
            To plan trips, the app asks public online services for routes, places, weather and fuel prices; section 4
            lists each of them and what it receives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">2. Who is responsible</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            LPG Trip Planner is a personal app by Arash Kadkhodaei, Freiburg, Germany (postal address in the <a href="/impressum/" class={link}>Impressum</a>).
            Email: <a href="mailto:arash@kadkhodaei.de" class={link}>arash@kadkhodaei.de</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">3. What stays on your phone</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Everything the app records or you enter is stored in the app’s private storage on your phone: drives (with
            their start and end points), fill-ups, tank levels, saved trips, your places (Home, favourites, recent
            places), prices, links to station price pages, recent place-search results, settings and any API keys you
            enter. GPS tracks are recorded only if you turn on “Record GPS tracks” in the developer options; those files
            stay on the phone and are never uploaded or backed up.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-muted-foreground mt-3 space-y-1">
            <li>
              <strong class={strong}>Location</strong> is used to plan from where you are and to record drives. In the
              background it is used only while Android Auto is connected, and for at most two minutes after your car’s
              Bluetooth device connects while the app waits for Android Auto.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class={strong}>Bluetooth (nearby devices)</strong> is used only to recognise the one car device you
              pair. The app keeps that device’s name and address on the phone, reads nothing else over Bluetooth and sends
              nothing.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class={strong}>Notifications</strong> show a running drive and questions such as whether you filled
              up.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">4. Online services the app contacts</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The app talks to these services directly from your phone. Like any internet connection, each request shows
            the service your phone’s IP address. Apart from the Google Drive backup (section 7), no request carries an
            account, your name or a device identifier. What each service does with a request is governed by its own
            privacy policy.
          </p>
          <ul class="text-muted-foreground mt-4 space-y-4">
            {services.map(({ name, sends, policies }) => (
              <li>
                <strong class={strong}>{name}</strong>
                <br />
                {sends}
                <br />
                Privacy policy:{' '}
                {policies.map(({ label, href }, i) => (
                  <>
                    {i > 0 && ', '}
                    <a href={href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>{label}</a>
                  </>
                ))}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">5. Calendar</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Only if you allow it, the app reads the events of the next 14 days that have a place, to suggest trips to
            them. Only an event’s place is sent to the map search (Photon, then Nominatim), never its title or guests.
            Titles, times, notes and guests stay on the phone, and search results are kept on the phone for 30 days. You
            can turn this off, or leave out single calendars, in the app’s settings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">6. Receipt scanning</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A photo of a pump receipt is read on the phone by Google’s ML Kit text recognition, using the model built into
            the app, offline. The photo is deleted right after it is read. The app has no camera permission (your
            camera app takes the photo), and ML Kit’s component for sending usage logs is removed from the app, so
            nothing about your receipts is sent to Google or anyone else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">7. Backups</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <strong class={strong}>Google Drive (optional, from version 1.3.0).</strong> Only if you turn it on, Google
            asks you to choose an account and to allow the app access. The app asks only for
            the <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-muted px-1 rounded">drive.appdata</code> permission: it stores a backup file
            of the app’s data (drives, fill-ups, tank levels, saved trips, prices and settings, without your API keys),
            plus the backup before it, in the hidden app-data folder of your own Google Drive. That folder is hidden from
            you and other apps, only this app can read it, and the app cannot see any of your other Drive files. The
            backup goes directly from your phone to Google; it never passes through me.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3">
            Data obtained from Google APIs is used only to back up your app data and restore it to your phone. It is never
            shared with anyone, sold, or used for advertising or any other purpose. LPG Trip Planner’s use and transfer to
            any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere to <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
            including the Limited Use requirements.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3">
            To delete the Drive backup, turn the Drive backup off in the app and choose to delete it there. You can also
            delete it in Google Drive on a computer, under Settings › Manage apps › LPG Trip Planner › Options, where
            Google offers to delete the app’s hidden data. You can remove the app’s access at any time in your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Google Account</a>;
            after that, the app can no longer read or write the backup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3">
            <strong class={strong}>Backup file (optional, from version 1.3.0).</strong> If you choose a folder, the app
            writes the same backup file there. The file is then yours: whoever provides that storage keeps it, and you
            can delete it like any other file.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3">
            <strong class={strong}>Android backup.</strong> If backup is turned on for your phone, Android may store the
            app’s data in your Google account, as Android allows for every app. The settings file (with your API keys),
            the current plan and GPS track files are left out. A direct transfer to a new phone copies everything except
            the GPS track files.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">8. Deleting your data</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Uninstalling the app deletes its data on your phone. From version 1.3.0, Android 10 and later ask whether to
            keep the app’s data when you uninstall; if you keep it, it stays on the phone for a later reinstall. Within the
            app you can delete single fill-ups and clear downloaded prices at any time. Delete backup files yourself, and the Drive backup as described in section 7. Because I receive no data from the
            app, I hold nothing about you that I could show, correct or delete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">9. Children</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">The app is meant for drivers and is not directed at children.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">10. Changes to this policy</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            If the app starts using another service or handles your data differently, I update this page and its date
            first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 text-foreground">11. Contact</h2>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Questions about this policy or the app: <a href="mailto:arash@kadkhodaei.de" class={link}>arash@kadkhodaei.de</a>
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
